-pound bomb known as MOAB, the largest nonnuclear weapon in the U.S. arsena=
l, would probably not destroy an underground site like Pickaxe, which is co=
mposed of granite.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The campaign would inflict more damage =
on Iran=E2=80=99s already degraded missile and drone arsenal, and could per=
haps result in concessions in limiting the country=E2=80=99s nuclear progra=
m and fully reopening the Strait of Hormuz. But such an approach has not wo=
rked before. Iran would strike back at Gulf states, almost certainly drivin=
g up energy prices and inflation. Defending the Gulf states would strain th=
e Pentagon=E2=80=99s eroded global supply of missile interceptors.</span></=
p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15p=
x"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Pared-back strikes</span></p><p style=3D=
"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>A sh=
orter, scaled-back version of the first plan would probably take many energ=
y targets off the table, focusing on military and nuclear sites, officials =
said. The thinking behind this approach is that Iran would be less likely t=
o retaliate by attacking Gulf energy infrastructure.</span></p><p style=3D"=
font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Trump=
 could claim that the U.S. had inflicted additional damage on Iran=E2=80=99=
s arsenal, declare victory and begin the exodus from the region. A more lim=
ited offensive would also preserve the Pentagon=E2=80=99s stockpile of scar=
ce interceptors.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;=
color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But administration officials concede that=
 a limited strike would achieve none of the goals Trump set out at the begi=
nning of the war, and would still leave Iran with significant leverage over=
 the Strait of Hormuz.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cell=
