ieve Trump would be lucky to get limits akin to those in the Obama-era deal=
, which Trump withdrew from in 2018.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:=
700">Muddle along</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif=
;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In this scenario, the U.S. would live wi=
th the status quo: an expensive naval blockade that prevents Iran from expo=
rting oil, while U.S. escorts and patrols ensure that 11 to 18 million barr=
els of non-Iranian oil get through each day. This would effectively allow T=
rump to avoid making a decision, in the hopes of a longer-term resolution.<=
/span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin=
:0 0 15px"><span>He could also seek to negotiate a narrow deal to reopen th=
e Strait of Hormuz, leave sanctions in place and slowly withdraw =E2=80=94 =
letting the Gulf Arabs work out a deal with the Iranians over how the strai=
t is managed.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;col=
or:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But such a deal would not address the nuclea=
r or missile issues =E2=80=94 much less the nature of the Iranian leadershi=
p =E2=80=94 that led Trump to go to war in February. And there would be no =
